eratorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes the hyperboloid of one sheet defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=16.$ By expressing the equation of the hyperboloid in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the second component of $f.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola ${r}^{2}-{z}^{2}={k}^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨r,z⟩=⟨k\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,k\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ\\in R.$" 13194139536124 true] [17592186176118 1100 "The vector-valued function $f(θ, ϕ)$, given by\n\n$f(θ, ϕ) = ⟨4\\cosh ϕ\\cos θ, _____, 4\\sinh ϕ⟩$\n\nparametrizes the hyperboloid of one sheet defined in Cartesian coordinates as $x^{2} + y^{2} - z^{2} = 16$. By expressing the equation of the hyperboloid in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the second component of $f$.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola $r^{2} - z^{2} = k^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨r, z⟩ = ⟨k\\cosh ϕ, k\\sinh ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ ∈ R$." 13194139536124 false] [17592186176118 1145 "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =16 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{z}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-{z}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola ${r}^{2}-{z}^{2}=16$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta $ with the positive $x$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n⟨r,z⟩=⟨4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩,\\,ϕ\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,(4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$" 13194139536124 true] [17592186176119 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139536124 true] [17592186176119 1100 "The vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ),$ given by \n\n$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes the hyperboloid of one sheet defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-{y}^{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=1.\n$$\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=3X,\\,y=Z,\\,z=2Y\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the first component of $f.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola ${r}^{2}-{Z}^{2}={k}^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨r,Z⟩=⟨k\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,k\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ\\in R.$" 13194139536124 true] [17592186176119 1100 "The vector-valued function $f(θ, ϕ)$, given by\n\n$f(θ, ϕ) = ⟨_____, \\sinh ϕ, 2\\cosh ϕ\\sin θ⟩$\n\nparametrizes the hyperboloid of one sheet defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n\n$\\frac{x^{2}}{9} - y^{2} + \\frac{z^{2}}{4} = 1$.\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n$x = 3X, y = Z, z = 2Y$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the first component of $f$.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola $r^{2} - Z^{2} = k^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨r, Z⟩ = ⟨k\\cosh ϕ, k\\sinh ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ ∈ R$." 13194139536124 false] [17592186176119 1145 "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=3X,\\,y=Z,\\,z=2Y\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\n{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=Z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola ${r}^{2}-{Z}^{2}=1$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $Z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta $ with the positive $X$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n⟨r,Z⟩=⟨\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩,\\,ϕ\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3X,Z,2Y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z,\\,2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ,\\,2(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$" 13194139536124 true] [17592186176124 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536124 true] [17592186176124 1100 "$$\nf(y,z)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,y,\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(y,z)$ above parametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $5x=10{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2}.$ What is the first component of $f?$" 13194139536124 true] [17592186176124 1100 "$f(y, z) = ⟨_____, y, z⟩$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(y, z)$ above parametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $5x = 10y^{2} + 2z^{2}$. What is the first component of $f$?" 13194139536124 false] [17592186176124 1145 "Solving our equation for $x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n5x=10{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2}\\,⟹\\,x=2{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{5}{z}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{5}{z}^{2},\\,y,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $2{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{5}{z}^{2}.$" 13194139536124 true] [17592186176323 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139536124 true] [17592186176323 1100 "The vector-valued function $f(r, θ)$, given by\n\n$f(r, θ) = ⟨r^{2}, 2r\\cos θ, _____⟩$\n\nparametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n$x = \\frac{y^{2}}{4} + z^{2}$.\nBy first considering the transformations\n$x = Z, y = 2X, z = Y$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the third component of $f$." 13194139536124 false] [17592186176323 1100 "The vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta ),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨{r}^{2},\\,2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+{z}^{2}.\n$$\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=Z,\\,y=2X,\\,z=Y\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the third component of $f.$" 13194139536124 true] [17592186176323 1145 "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=Z,\\,y=2X,\\,z=Y\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get \n\n$$\nZ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2} \\\\ Z & =(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨Z,2X,Y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨Z,\\,2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{r}^{2},\\,2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$" 13194139536124 true] [17592186384675 1017 #edb/ref 17592186384676 13194139536124 true] [17592186384675 1089 "q-164463" 13194139536124 true] [17592186384675 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536124 true] [17592186384675 1100 "$$\nf(x,y)=⟨x,\\,y,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(x,y)$ above parametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\frac{z}{2}=3{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}.$ What is the third component of $f?$" 13194139536124 true] [17592186384675 1113 #uuid "3312d10b-5f81-5bae-a433-29944a392e91" 13194139536124 true] [17592186384675 1145 "Solving our equation for $z,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{z}{2}=3{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\,⟹\\,z=6{x}^{2}+8{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,6{x}^{2}+8{y}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $6{x}^{2}+8{y}^{2}.$" 13194139536124 true] [17592186384676 1000 #edb/ref 17592186384677 13194139536124 true] [17592186384676 1000 #edb/ref 17592186384678 13194139536124 tr